From 391a0f126837536db8f63b7549d97a66b34f3d1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 20:38:45 -0700
Subject: [RFC net-next v1 5/5] net: flow_dissector: descend into FOU/GUE inner
 flow
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add opt-in inner-flow descent for Foo-over-UDP (FOU) and Generic UDP
Encapsulation (GUE), the UDP encapsulations added by Tom Herbert.

Unlike VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U, FOU and GUE have no well-known UDP port, so
the dissector cannot identify them from the packet alone — it must map
the UDP destination port to its configured encapsulation. That config
lives in the (tristate) fou module, so a struct flow_dissector_fou_ops
is registered by fou and consulted (under RCU) by the dissector only
when a gate is on:

  - GUE (net.flow_dissector.gue_inner) is self-describing: once the port
    resolves to a GUE tunnel, guehdr->proto_ctype gives the inner IP
    protocol. Only version-0 data messages with an IPIP/IPv6 inner are
    descended; control messages, unknown versions/flags and non-IP
    inners defer.
  - Direct FOU (net.flow_dissector.fou_inner) is bare: the inner
    protocol is not on the wire, it is per-tunnel config, so the fou
    table supplies both "this is FOU" and the inner IP protocol.

The descent reuses the IPv4/IPv6 fast helpers for the inner header and
is bounded by the same num_hdrs limit as the other tunnel descents; it
stamps FLOW_DIS_ENCAPSULATION. Both are behaviour-changing (inner vs
outer identity), gated off by default, and never auto-managed — same
posture as the other *_inner knobs.

fou_core: fou_list add/del switch to the _rcu variants (entries were
already kfree_rcu()'d) so the dissector can walk it locklessly from the
packet path; fou registers the ops on init and unregisters on exit.

RFC / design questions for the list:
  - Is a per-packet fou-table walk on the UDP path acceptable, or should
    the mapping be indexed (e.g. a port hash)? It is gated by a
    static_branch so it is zero-cost when off.
  - Direct FOU dissection is fundamentally stateful (inner protocol is
    not in the packet); is that in scope for the stateless dissector, or
    should only self-describing GUE descend?
  - Same tunnel-port-discovery question as vxlan_inner: the mapping here
    is exact (from the fou table) rather than a hardcoded port, which is
    arguably the right model for the others too.

Signed-off-by: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
